Question 5: Software Maintenance, Reengineering, and AI in Software Engineering

1. Introduction to Software Maintenance

Software maintenance is the process of modifying, updating, and improving existing software after its deployment. It involves correcting errors, improving performance, updating features, and adapting the software to changing user requirements and technological environments.

In the case of the college’s Student Management System (SMS), software maintenance is necessary to improve its reliability, efficiency, security, and overall functionality.

2. Identification of Problems in the Existing System

The major problems identified in the existing Student Management System are:

* Slow Performance: The system takes excessive time to process student records and retrieve information.
* Outdated Features: Existing functionalities do not meet modern student and faculty requirements.
* Frequent Errors: Software bugs cause incorrect results and system failures.
* Poor User Interface: The outdated interface may make navigation difficult.
* Security Risks: Older software technologies may contain security vulnerabilities.
* Poor Maintainability: Outdated code can make future modifications difficult.

3. Suitable Types of Software Maintenance

Maintenance Type	Description	Application in SMS
Corrective Maintenance	Fixes existing bugs and errors.	Correcting errors in attendance, marks, and student records.
Adaptive Maintenance	Adapts software to new technological environments.	Supporting modern operating systems, browsers, and databases.
Perfective Maintenance	Improves performance and functionality.	Improving loading speed, user interface, and adding new features.
Preventive Maintenance	Prevents potential future problems.	Refactoring code, updating security, and removing outdated components.

All four types of maintenance can be applied to improve the existing Student Management System.

4. Steps Involved in Software Reengineering

Software reengineering is the process of analyzing and transforming an existing software system to improve its quality and maintainability while preserving its essential functionality.

Step 1: Inventory Analysis

Identify existing software modules, databases, features, and technologies that require modernization.

Step 2: Document Restructuring

Update outdated technical documentation, system requirements, and user manuals.

Step 3: Reverse Engineering

Analyze the existing source code and system architecture to understand how the software works.

Step 4: Code Restructuring

Remove unnecessary code, simplify complex functions, fix errors, and improve code readability.

Step 5: Data Restructuring

Optimize the database, remove duplicate records, and migrate existing student information securely to a modern database.

Step 6: Forward Engineering

Rebuild and modernize the system using updated technologies, improved architecture, and modern user interfaces.

Step 7: Testing and Deployment

Perform functional, performance, and security testing before deploying the modernized system.

5. Applications of AI in Software Engineering

A. AI-Assisted Bug Detection and Code Analysis

AI-powered tools can analyze existing source code to identify bugs, security vulnerabilities, inefficient algorithms, and coding errors.

Applications:

* Detecting errors in attendance and examination modules.
* Suggesting code improvements.
* Identifying potential security vulnerabilities.
* Improving code quality and performance.

Example Tools: GitHub Copilot and DeepCode (Snyk Code).

B. AI-Assisted Software Testing

AI can assist in automatically generating test cases, predicting potential software failures, and identifying defects.

Applications:

* Generating test cases for student registration and login.
* Testing attendance and marks calculation.
* Detecting unexpected system behavior.
* Reducing manual testing effort.

Example Tools: Testim and GitHub Copilot.

AI-generated code and test cases should be reviewed by developers, and confidential student information must be protected.

6. Three Expected Improvements

Improvement	Expected Outcome
Improved Performance	Faster loading, optimized database queries, and reduced response time.
Improved Reliability and Security	Fewer software errors, reduced system failures, and better protection of student information.
Improved Maintainability and Usability	Cleaner source code, easier future updates, and a modern user-friendly interface.

